# ============================================================
#  Baseline pools (phase 3, step 1b): course mornings for
#  Illusion & Glamour, Beastcraft & Flight and Umbral Theory.
#  Repeatable routine events, drawn once per enrolled course a day.
# ============================================================

# ---------------- Illusion & Glamour (track: illusion) ----------------

@event illu_candle_drill
title: The Candle Drill
track: illusion
level: 1
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: candle
tags: class magic
scene: A row of unlit candles on a lecture bench, each crowned with a flame that casts no shadow, a professor holding her palm over one.
text: {Illusion I opens with a row of cold candles. Make them burn, Professor Vey says, without burning.|Vey's drawer of candles is open. Every one is older than you.|The candle drill. Some mornings you could do it asleep.} {Vey passes her hand through each flame and says "cold" in the same flat voice.|Someone's flame leans in a draught only it can feel.|The student beside you has made a lovely candle and forgotten the flame.}
> left: Hold the flame until it's right
  study illusion +small
  mana -small
  say: {Your flame steadies, then flickers in time with your breathing, which Vey says is the point.|Vey's palm hovers over your flame a moment longer than the others. "Almost warm," she says. From her, that's a sonnet.|By the bell your flame has a proper blue heart. You feel oddly proud of something that isn't there.}
> right: Make it gutter and smoke
  challenge deceive using charm standard:
    win:
      charm +small
      mood +tiny
      say: {You add a thread of smoke and a little gutter. The student behind you reaches to pinch it out.|Your candle drips wax that isn't there onto a desk that is. Vey watches it fall and says nothing, which means yes.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      say: {The smoke goes up and the flame goes out, as though embarrassed by its own ambition.|Vey waves a hand through the whole performance. "A candle doesn't try this hard," she says.}

@event illu_one_small_change
title: One Small Change
track: illusion
level: 1
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: mirror
tags: class magic
scene: Students bent over little hand mirrors, one changing the colour of her own eyes, another squinting at a nose that isn't quite his.
text: {Hand mirrors all round. Glamour your own face, Vey says; change one small thing and hold it.|Out come the mirrors. A glamour you can't hold on yourself, Vey says, won't hold on anyone.|Mirror practice. You know your own face too well by now.} {Vey says people always change their eyes first.|A second-year from Rootward is giving himself a better chin, slowly.|The mirrors fog, as they do {season == "winter": in the cold|when the room warms}.}
> left: Change something trivial
  study illusion +small
  say: {You move a freckle half an inch. It takes the whole hour. Vey initials your page without looking up.|You darken one eyebrow and keep it dark. Dull, careful work, and the glamour holds.|A small scar vanishes and comes back, vanishes and comes back. By the end it stays gone until you let it.}
> right: Change what you'd really change
  charm +small
  mood -small
  say: {It holds beautifully. You look at it longer than the exercise requires, and let it go more slowly than you'd like.|Vey glances over your shoulder. "Ah," she says, kindly enough. "That one. Everybody has one."|You hold it all hour. Afterwards your real face seems slightly unfamiliar, like a coat you've lent out.}

@event illu_spot_the_coin
title: Spot the Coin
track: illusion
level: 1
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: coin
tags: class social
cast:
  partner: any where tagged(partner, student)
scene: Two students at a bench with three coins between them, one coin shimmering very slightly at the edge.
text: {Partner work. {partner} lays out three crowns, and one of them is an illusion. Say which.|Three crowns from {partner}. One isn't there, and {they} look{s} pleased about it.|You and {partner} have started keeping score.} {Vey walks the room flicking real coins, to confuse everyone.|The windows are bright and the illusions show it.|The next bench is arguing whether a coin can be "partly fake".}
> left: Study the coins honestly
  study illusion +small
  rel partner +tiny
  say: {It's the one with no tarnish. Illusions always come out cleaner than money does.|You stare until your eyes water and pick the middle one. Wrong. {partner} pockets the real two, laughing.|You get it on the third try, by the shine that doesn't move when the light does.}
> right: Watch their face, not the coins
  challenge deceive using charm standard:
    win:
      charm +small
      rel partner +tiny
      say: {{partner} glances at the left coin a fraction too fondly. You point. {They} groan{s}.|You keep smiling until {partner} gives it away by smiling back. Vey notes that this isn't the exercise, and that it worked.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      say: {{partner} has a better face than you'd thought. Your wrong guess buys {them} a cup of tea.|You watch {partner} so hard you miss the coin blinking out of existence entirely.}

@event illu_bread_smell
title: The Smell of Bread
track: illusion
level: 2
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: meal
tags: class magic
scene: A lecture bench with an illusory loaf of bread on a board, steam rising from it, and several students sniffing it doubtfully.
text: {Illusion II adds the other senses. Today, a loaf that looks, smells and sounds fresh when broken.|More bread. Sight is easy, Vey says; smell is where liars are caught.|The bread lesson. The floor smells of loaves that never existed.} {Somebody's loaf smells strongly of wet dog.|One loaf crackles beautifully and smells of nothing.|{gpa >= 3: Vey asks you to go first.|Vey asks you to go last, which feels pointed.}}
> left: Get the smell right
  study illusion +small
  say: {You spend an hour on the smell of a crust. By the end it's nearly convincing and you're desperately hungry.|You think of a bakery on a cold street and the smell arrives with it. Vey sniffs, and nods once.|The smell is right. The sound of breaking is wrong. Vey says that's the usual order.}
> right: Add the warmth of the oven
  challenge enchant using charm standard:
    win:
      charm +small
      renown +tiny
      say: {Vey picks your loaf up and puts it down hastily, as if it had burnt her. The class actually applauds.|The bench beside you swears the room got warmer. It didn't. That's the point.}
    lose:
      mana -small
      mood -tiny
      say: {The warmth arrives and the loaf leaves. You're left holding a hot, empty board.|Your bread steams, then smokes, then smells of a cupboard. Vey writes something short.}

@event illu_find_vey
title: Find the Professor
track: illusion
level: 2
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: mask
tags: class magic
scene: A lecture hall where the professor's chair is empty and everyone is looking suspiciously at everyone else, including a pigeon on the sill.
text: {Professor Vey is rarely at the lectern in Illusion II. She is somewhere in the room, wearing someone else, and the first to name her wins.|Vey's chair is empty. Everyone eyes the porter with the mop.|The lectern is empty. You trust nothing in this room.} {There's a pigeon on the sill.|A student nobody remembers has taken the back row.|One of the portraits seems unusually attentive.}
> left: Watch everyone, all lesson
  study illusion +small
  mood -small
  say: {You don't find her, but you notice who blinks late. Vey turns out to have been the pigeon.|You learn three classmates' nervous habits and nothing about Vey, who was the chair.|At the end she steps out of the porter's coat. You'd suspected the mop. The mop was real.}
> right: Name her early
  challenge deceive using charm standard+2:
    win:
      charm +small
      mood +tiny
      say: {"Professor," you say to the new student in the back row. The glamour falls away like a dropped shawl. Vey looks almost pleased.|You name her before the bell has finished ringing. She says, "Hm," which from Vey means a great deal.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      say: {You accuse a perfectly ordinary classmate. He will remember it. So will Vey, who was standing behind you.|You point at the portrait. The portrait points back. Vey, at the lectern after all, makes a note.}

@event illu_courtyard_hour
title: Holding the Courtyard
track: illusion
level: 3
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: sparkle
tags: class magic danger
scene: A student standing very still in a busy courtyard, sweat on their brow, while a flock of illusory swallows circles above the crowd.
text: {Advanced Illusion leaves the classroom. Hold an illusion over the busy courtyard for an hour, and don't let it fray.|Another courtyard hour. The porters step round you now.|The courtyard. {final_year: One of the last times you'll hold this sky.|Your hands know the weight of it.}} {A flock of swallows today.|Rain that doesn't wet anyone.|{season == "winter": Snow that never lands.|{season == "spring": Blossom that never lands.|{season == "autumn": Leaves that never land.|Thistledown that never lands.}}}}
> left: Hold it the full hour
  charm +small
  health -small
  say: {By the end your head rings like a struck bell. Nobody looked up once, which is how you know it worked.|A first-year holds out a hand to catch something you made. You keep it falling for her until the bell.|You hold it, then sit on the fountain's edge for longer than you'd like anyone to see.}
> right: Keep it small and steady
  study illusion +small
  say: {You keep only a corner of it, perfect, for an hour. Vey says ambition is cheap and an hour is not.|You shrink it to a patch of weather over one bench. It never frays, and nobody notices, which is the point and the pity.}

@event illu_original_work
title: An Illusion of Your Own
track: illusion
level: 3
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: eye
tags: class magic
scene: A darkened studio room, a student alone at the centre, and around them the half-formed image of a lit window at dusk.
text: {Original work, an illusion nobody taught you. Vey asks one question of each piece. "What does it make people feel?"|More original work. Vey's question hasn't changed; your answers get longer.|Studio morning. {final_year: Next year, Vey says, she'll ask someone else.|Vey goes round the room asking her question.}} {A classmate has made a door that sighs.|Someone has made the smell of rain on hot stone.|Someone's illusion is humming.}
> left: Make something true
  study illusion +small
  mood -small
  say: {You make the window of a house you grew up near, at dusk. Vey stands in front of it a long time and writes nothing down.|You make a corridor that remembers who walked down it. It's sadder than you meant. Vey says that's usually the good sign.}
> right: Make something dazzling
  challenge enchant using charm standard+1 steady:
    win:
      charm +small
      renown +small
      say: {A staircase of light climbs into the ceiling and keeps going. The class goes quiet. "Yes," says Vey. "But what does it feel like?"|Your piece fills the room with lanterns, one for each person watching. Vey counts them, and finds one for herself.}
    lose:
      mana -small
      mood -small
      say: {It's dazzling for a breath and then empty, like a fair after the fair has gone.|Vey watches it all, politely. "Very bright," she says. You'd have preferred anything else.}

# ---------------- Beastcraft & Flight (track: beastcraft) ----------------

@event beast_mucking_out
title: Mucking Out
track: beastcraft
level: 1
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: paw
tags: class nature
scene: A student with a pitchfork in a straw-strewn stable, a hippogriff watching from its stall with a look of deep judgement.
text: {Beastcraft I begins in the stables, with a pitchfork. Professor Brask says you don't touch a creature until you've cleaned up after it.|Pitchforks. Brask still hasn't said when that rule ends.|The stables, early. You know which stall to start with.} {The hippogriff in the end stall is watching you.|Owls shift in the rafters, unimpressed.|{season == "summer": The heat doubles the smell.|A cold wind finds every gap.}}
> left: Muck out properly
  vigor +small
  mood -small
  say: {Two stalls, a barrow and a blister. Brask inspects your work and calls the straw "nearly decent".|You finish before anyone else. The hippogriff treads straight through your clean straw, deliberately.|Brask says nothing, which you've learned is the best review she gives humans.}
> right: Bow to the hippogriff first
  challenge feat standard:
    win:
      study beastcraft +small
      mood +tiny
      say: {You bow low and hold it. The hippogriff considers, then bows back. Brask writes your name on the board, without comment.|The hippogriff lets you near enough to see where its feathers turn to fur. You'll remember the exact line.}
    lose:
      health -small
      say: {You bow too fast. The hippogriff takes it as a challenge and accepts.|The hippogriff looks at your bow, then at your hat, and shoves you into the barrow to get at it. It eats the hat.}

@event beast_hover_line
title: The Hover Line
track: beastcraft
level: 1
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: broom
tags: class danger
scene: A line of students hovering a foot above a lawn on brooms, one broom drifting out of line, a stern professor counting slowly.
text: {Flight is half the course. Brask lines you up on the lawn to hover a foot up and hold still while she counts.|The hover line. Brask counts slowly on purpose, you're sure.|Hover line. You and your broom have an understanding, of sorts.} {Somebody's broom keeps edging toward the lake.|An owl has landed on the end of a broom, and its rider is pretending not to notice.|Brask says, as always, that brooms are harder to reason with than griffons.}
> left: Hold still until the count
  vigor +small
  health -tiny
  say: {You hold it until forty. Your legs remember it for a day.|The broom bucks once at thirty-one. You hold on. Brask gets to fifty and nods.|Steady all the way. Brask says it's nice to see a broom that knows who's in charge, and she means the broom.}
> right: Study how the others fall off
  study beastcraft +small
  mood -tiny
  say: {You stand at the end and watch. You learn a great deal about balance, and nobody lets you forget you didn't fly.|You take notes on every wobble. Brask reads them and says you should consider a career in inquests.}

@event beast_feeding_round
title: Feeding Round
track: beastcraft
level: 1
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: feather
tags: class nature
cast:
  partner: any where tagged(partner, student)
scene: Two students carrying buckets between stalls, a sulky griffon chick in a nest of straw, owls leaning down from the rafters.
text: {Feeding round, in pairs. You and {partner} have two buckets and a list, and the griffon chick is first on it.|Feeding round with {partner}. The griffon chick has heard the buckets.|You and {partner} could do the round blindfolded now. The chick would still sulk.} {Brask has written "DO NOT HURRY" at the top of the list.|The owls lean down from the rafters to watch.|{partner} is humming to the buckets.}
> left: Take the griffon chick
  challenge feat standard wild:
    win:
      vigor +small
      rel partner +tiny
      say: {The chick eats from the bucket, then from your hand, then tries your sleeve. {partner} applauds quietly.|It sulks, then eats, then sulks about having eaten. Brask says that's progress for a griffon.}
    lose:
      health -small
      say: {The chick bites the bucket, then the hand holding it. {partner} finishes the round alone, kindly.|It knocks the bucket over, and you with it, and sits in it, glaring. You spend the hour, bruised and soaked, fishing out its breakfast.}
> right: Take the owls instead
  study beastcraft +small
  rel partner +tiny
  say: {You learn the owls' names from the tags on their perches, and what a pellet is, in detail.|Up and down the ladder for an hour. {partner} survives the chick and is grateful in a quiet way.|The owls take their mice with great ceremony. One of them seems to be counting yours.}

@event beast_hare_paddock
title: Running with Hares
track: beastcraft
level: 2
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: bolt
tags: class magic
scene: Students sprinting across a paddock alongside a scattering of brown hares, one student's feet suddenly much faster than the rest of them.
text: {Beastcraft II teaches haste the old way. Run with the hares until their speed rubs off. "Borrow it," Brask says. "Don't steal it."|The hare paddock. The hares remember you.|Hares. You dream in sudden right angles now.} {The grass is wet and the hares don't care.|Brask times you with a crooked hourglass.|{season == "summer": It's hot and the hares are lazy.|The paddock smells of rain.}}
> left: Run until it catches
  vigor +small
  health -small
  say: {For three strides you're faster than you've any right to be. Then you're in the hedge.|You catch it on the fourth lap, a lightness behind the knees. You pay for it with a nap you can't refuse.|The hares let you keep up for a moment, out of curiosity. It's the best moment of your week.}
> right: Watch the hares and take notes
  study beastcraft +small
  say: {You sketch the way a hare turns, everything at once, no warning. Brask says your sketch is better than your running.|You note how the hares rest between bursts. Brask reads it and says, "Yes. That's the half everyone forgets."}

@event beast_complaints
title: The Complaints Hour
track: beastcraft
level: 2
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: owl
tags: class nature
scene: A student sitting on an upturned bucket in a stable, surrounded by animals who all seem to be talking at once.
text: {Beast-speech practice. You sit on a bucket in the stables and listen. Every creature has been waiting to complain, and the owls start with the draught.|Another hour on the bucket. The owls open, as always, with the draught.|The bucket, the owls, the draught. Brask calls it fieldwork.} {The hippogriff wants different hay. It is very specific.|The griffon chick has a list.|{gpa >= 3: Brask leaves you alone with them, which feels like trust.|Brask stays near the door, which feels like supervision.}}
> left: Hear every complaint out
  study beastcraft +small
  mood -small
  say: {The owls talk for forty minutes about the draught. You understand every word now, which is the trouble.|The hippogriff explains its views on hay at length. You come away fluent and exhausted.|A mouse you didn't know was there wants to be on the record. You put it on the record.}
> right: Fix the draught yourself
  vigor +small
  health -tiny
  say: {You climb into the rafters with a hammer and a plank. The owls supervise. The draught stops, and so do the complaints, briefly.|You patch the gap with straw and a board. The owls say thank you, then mention the window.}

@event beast_lake_flight
title: Over the Lake
track: beastcraft
level: 3
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 4
weight: 10
lesson: mirror_lake
art: feather
tags: class danger
scene: A student on a hippogriff's back skimming low over a mirror-still lake, their reflection racing along beneath them.
text: {Advanced Beastcraft. A hippogriff each, over the lake, no saddle unless you ask. Brask won't think less of you for asking. She'll just remember.|The lake flight. Your hippogriff knows the way.|Over the lake. {final_year: You catch yourself memorising the view.|Your reflection keeps pace.}} {The water is perfectly still.|A barge below, its passengers waving.|{season == "summer": The air is full of midges.|The wind off the water stings.}}
> left: Ride bareback, low over the water
  challenge feat standard+1:
    win:
      vigor +small
      mood +small
      say: {You skim so low a wingtip draws a line across the lake. For one breath you and your reflection touch hands.|The hippogriff banks and you go with it, as if you'd always known how. Brask says nothing at all, then grins.}
    lose:
      health -small
      mood -tiny
      say: {The hippogriff dips and you don't. The lake is colder than it looks. It always is.|You slide off on the turn and hang on by a fistful of feathers, all the way back to shore.}
> right: Take the saddle and the high circuit
  study beastcraft +small
  say: {Saddled, high and steady. You see all seven towers at once, and nobody sees you.|The safe circuit, twice. Brask remembers, as promised, and nods at you all the same.}

@event beast_manticore_rounds
title: Manticore Rounds
track: beastcraft
level: 3
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: paw
tags: class danger
scene: A manticore lying in straw with its eyes half-closed, one student gently holding its scorpion tail while another hums nearby.
text: {Advanced students check the manticore once a month. Someone holds the tail; someone hums the lullaby. Brask lets you choose.|Manticore rounds. It knows the lullaby better than you do.|The manticore yawns at you, which is either a welcome or a threat.} {Its tail twitches in its sleep.|It has been off its food, Brask says, and is in a mood.|{final_year: You wonder who'll hold the tail after you.|Brask has brought a bucket of meat and a smaller one of patience.}}
> left: Hold the tail
  challenge feat standard+1 wild:
    win:
      vigor +small
      renown +tiny
      say: {You hold the tail still while Brask checks the sting. It's warm and heavy and does not, at any point, stab you.|The manticore lets you hold its tail the way a cat lets you hold a paw. Under protest, briefly, as a favour.}
    lose:
      health -small
      say: {The tail flicks. You let go just in time, mostly. Sister Amaranth asks no questions; she's seen this course before.|It rolls over, tail and all, onto you. Nothing breaks, but you're bruised in the exact shape of a manticore.}
> right: Hum the lullaby
  study beastcraft +small
  say: {You hum the whole lullaby twice, off-key on the high bit. The manticore sleeps through its own check-up.|You hum. A classmate holds the tail. Brask says the humming is the harder job, and nobody believes her.}

# ---------------- Umbral Theory (track: umbral) ----------------

@event umbral_dim_lamps
title: Reading by Shadow
track: umbral
level: 1
class: routine
tone: eerie
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: lantern
tags: class mystery
scene: A dim lecture room at the top of a tower, objects hidden under cloths on a table, their shadows thrown long on the wall behind.
text: {Umbral Theory I, top of the tower, lamps low on purpose. Name what's under each cloth by its shadow alone.|The cloths are back. The lamps are as low as ever.|Shadow reading. The dark no longer seems strange, which the professor says is the first lesson.} {One shadow is slightly larger than its object.|The professor moves the lamp an inch, and every answer changes.|{season == "summer": It's warm outside, never up here.|Rain on the windows makes the shadows shiver.}}
> left: Read them patiently
  study umbral +small
  say: {A teapot, a skull, a folded letter addressed to you. You open it later. It's blank.|You name every shadow but one. Under that cloth there is nothing at all. The professor doesn't explain.|Slowly, you get them all. The shadows told you, the professor says; you only had to stop interrupting.}
> right: Slip in a false shadow
  challenge deceive standard:
    win:
      guile +small
      say: {You add the shadow of a cat to the wall. Three classmates write "cat". The professor writes your name, and underlines it.|Your false shadow passes all morning. The professor only catches it when it doesn't move with the lamp.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      say: {Your false shadow points the wrong way. The professor turns it round gently, like straightening a picture.|Your shadow-cat sits up and leaves. It wasn't meant to do that. The professor doesn't seem surprised.}

@event umbral_chalk_ward
title: Pale Shells
track: umbral
level: 1
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: shield
tags: class magic
scene: Students behind faint, pale bubbles of light as a professor flicks pieces of chalk at them from the front of a dim room.
text: {Ward drill, in pairs. One of you raises a pale shell over the other, and the professor lets the lamp-shadows loose along the floor to find the gap.|Pairs again. The professor's shadows have learned new tricks since last time.|The paired drill. You know how the shadows like to come: low, and from behind.} {Your partner's shell keeps flickering.|Your partner is a first-year who has never held a ward for more than a minute.|{gpa >= 3: The professor pairs you with the weakest student, on purpose.|The professor pairs you with someone as nervous as you are.}}
> left: Hold the shell over both of you
  study umbral +small
  mana -small
  say: {You stretch your shell over your partner as well. The shadows nose along its edge for an hour and find nothing. You feel every touch in your teeth.|Two people under one ward is twice the weight. Yours holds until the bell, just.|The shadows give up on your shell and go after the next pair. The professor nods at you, once.}
> right: Coach your partner through theirs
  guile +small
  say: {You talk your partner through it, breath by breath. Their shell holds for nine minutes, then a shadow slips under it and makes you both jump. "Teaching," says the professor, "is also a ward."|You keep your own shell down and watch for the gaps in theirs. By the end, they've stopped flickering. You've learned where shadows like to get in.}

@event umbral_quiet_minutes
title: The Quiet Minutes
track: umbral
level: 1
class: routine
tone: eerie
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: candle
tags: class mystery
scene: A circle of students sitting perfectly still in near darkness, a single candle in the middle, everyone listening very hard.
text: {Each lesson opens with ten minutes of silence. "Listen to what's hidden," the professor says, and puts out every lamp but one.|The quiet minutes. You can hear the tower settle, stone by stone.|Ten minutes of silence. You look forward to it, and also not.} {Someone's stomach rumbles at the worst moment.|The candle leans toward the door, though nothing comes in.|{season == "summer": A bee is lost in the room somewhere.|The wind hums in the stairwell.}}
> left: Listen properly
  study umbral +small
  mood -small
  say: {You hear the tower breathing, which it shouldn't, and a sigh on the stair that nobody makes.|You hear the lake, which is impossible from up here. The professor says that's normal for this room. It is not reassuring.|In the silence you hear someone you miss, very faintly. When the lamps come up, you don't mention it.}
> right: Quietly finish other homework
  challenge deceive adept-:
    win:
      guile +small
      say: {You finish a whole essay by feel in the dark. The professor never notices, or never says.|Not a scratch of the quill. When the lamps come up, your essay is done and your face is innocent.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      say: {When the lamps come up the professor is behind you, reading your essay. "Your third paragraph is weak," she says.|Your quill squeaks. In that silence it sounds like a scream. Everyone turns.}

@event umbral_short_distances
title: Short Distances
track: umbral
level: 2
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: ghost
tags: class magic danger
scene: A student stepping into the shadow under one desk and halfway out of the shadow under another, the room's lamps burning low.
text: {Umbral Theory II brings the shadowstep, desk to desk. "The shadows in between are not friendly," the professor says, "so don't linger."|Shadowstep drill. Three desks this time, then four.|The desks. You know the way between them, and the cold that lives there.} {Someone comes out under the wrong desk.|The lamps are lower than usual.|{gpa >= 3: The professor sets you the long way round.|The professor sets you the short way, and watches.}}
> left: Step across
  challenge deceive standard:
    win:
      guile +small
      say: {In, through, out. It takes no time and lasts forever. You come out under the far desk with frost on your sleeves.|Four desks, clean. Something in between brushes your ankle, curious, and lets you pass.}
    lose:
      health -small
      mood -tiny
      say: {You come out one desk short and somewhat upside down. The cold stays in your fingers until lunch.|You linger, just for a moment. It's enough. You come back pale, and the professor sits you by the one warm lamp.}
> right: Map the route on paper first
  study umbral +small
  say: {You draw every shadow in the room, and where it goes at each hour. The professor keeps your map.|You work out the route in ink and walk it once, slowly, in the light. It's dull, and correct.}

@event umbral_hidden_object
title: Present but Unseen
track: umbral
level: 2
class: routine
tone: eerie
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: key
tags: class mystery
scene: A dim classroom being searched by several students at once while one of them sits calmly at a desk with a small brass key in plain sight.
text: {Hide a small key in this room, present but unseen. "Hiding is not removal," the professor says. "Removal is for amateurs."|The key, and somewhere to hide it. Your classmates are better at finding things.|The key exercise. Half the class pats their pockets out of habit.} {The key is brass today.|The key is silver and very cold.|Last time a key hid in a shadow for two days.}
> left: Hide it, and stay calm
  challenge deceive standard:
    win:
      guile +small
      mood +tiny
      say: {You leave the key on the desk in plain sight, in your own shadow. Nobody sees it. The professor smiles, which is rarer than a key.|You hide it in the dark behind your own ear. They search the room twice and never once look at you.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      say: {A first-rate hiding place, found in under a minute by someone who simply asked the shadow.|You hide it so well you can't find it yourself. The professor retrieves it from the wall with a sigh.}
> right: Search for everyone else's
  study umbral +small
  say: {You find four keys, one in a hat, one in a hinge, one in a shadow that didn't want to let go.|You find one key and a pencil nobody has missed for years. The professor counts both.}

@event umbral_stair_wards
title: The Old Stair Wards
track: umbral
level: 3
class: routine
tone: eerie
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: shield
tags: class mystery
scene: A student kneeling on an ancient spiral stair, hands pressed to a faint ring of light set into the stone.
text: {Advanced students renew the small wards on the tower's old stair. Some are newer than the stone. Some, the professor says, are much older.|The stair wards. You know which step hums.|The stair. {final_year: Someone else will kneel here next year.|Your knees know every step.}} {One ward is warm, as if someone's just left.|Something beyond a ward leans back when you touch it.|The stair lamps are mostly a suggestion.}
> left: Renew them carefully
  study umbral +small
  mana -small
  say: {Step by step, ring by ring. Each ward takes a little of you and hums.|You renew eleven and pause at the twelfth. Leave that one, the professor says, and doesn't say whose it is.|At the top you're light-headed and the wards are bright. Something on the other side has gone quiet.}
> right: Study the oldest ward instead
  guile +small
  mood -small
  say: {You trace the oldest ring with one finger. The pattern folds in on itself like a knot nobody was meant to untie, and you feel watched for noticing even that.|The old ward is cold, careful work, made by someone who was afraid. You can feel the fear still in the stone.}

@event umbral_errand
title: The Shadow's Errand
track: umbral
level: 3
class: routine
tone: eerie
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: moon
tags: class magic mystery
scene: A student standing in lamplight with no shadow at all, while across the room their shadow lifts a book from a shelf.
text: {Original work. Send your own shadow on an errand across the room, the professor says, and have it come back. "It will want to wander. They all do."|Shadow errands. Yours picks its own route now.|Errand day. {final_year: Does your shadow know you're leaving soon?|Your shadow seems eager, which worries you.}} {Someone's shadow is still under a desk from last week.|The professor's own shadow is missing today.|The lamps are low and the shadows long.}
> left: Send it, and trust it
  challenge deceive standard+1:
    win:
      guile +small
      mood +tiny
      say: {Your shadow crosses the room, lifts a book, and brings it back open at a page you needed. You didn't ask it to.|It comes back with a feather from the rafters, which wasn't the errand. The professor counts it as initiative.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      health -tiny
      say: {It comes back an hour late and a little the wrong shape. For a day it lags behind you on stairs, sulking, and you can't get warm.|It doesn't come back until dusk. When it does, it smells of the lake. The professor makes you sit with the lamp until your hands stop shaking with cold.}
> right: Keep it on a short leash
  study umbral +small
  mana -small
  say: {Your shadow goes as far as the window and no further. It looks out a long time before it comes back.|Two yards and home. Dull, safe and precise. The professor says precision is how shadow-workers get old.}
